exec(open('/home/claude/work/devim/dmc.py').read())
C=2
V=[
VP(C,'2–3','devāsuramabhūdyuddhaṁ pūrṇamabdaśataṁ purā | / mahiṣe’surāṇāmadhipe devānāṁ ca purandare || 2|| / tatrāsurairmahāvīryairdevasainyaṁ parājitam | / jitvā ca sakalān devānindro’bhūnmahiṣāsuraḥ || 3||',
 'A HUNDRED YEARS OF WAR',
 sc('A buffalo-headed demon sitting on Indra’s throne in heaven while the gods are driven out below',BATTLE+'<rect x="80" y="40" width="60" height="50" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+BUFFALO(110,70,.7)+GODS(150,.45,5,40,35)+T(110,24,'100 YEARS',13)),
 'Long ago there was a war between gods and asuras for a full hundred years, when Mahiṣa was lord of the asuras and Indra (Purandara) of the gods. In it the gods’ army was defeated by the mighty asuras, and having conquered all the gods, Mahiṣāsura became Indra.',
 [('deva-asuram yuddham','<b>war of gods and asuras</b>'),('abda-śatam','<b>a hundred years</b>'),('purandara','<b>Indra</b>, “breaker of forts”'),('indraḥ abhūt','<b>became Indra</b>: took the throne of heaven')],
 '<b>The second carita begins.</b> The deity of this episode is <b>Mahālakṣmī</b>. “Indra” is a post, not just a person: whoever wins heaven sits on its throne. Now a buffalo demon holds it.',
 'Evil can win for a while; <b>Mahiṣa sits on heaven’s throne</b>.'),
VP(C,'9–10','itthaṁ niśamya devānāṁ vacāṁsi madhusūdanaḥ | / cakāra kopaṁ śambhuśca bhrukuṭīkuṭilānanau || 9|| / tato’tikopapūrṇasya cakriṇo vadanāttataḥ | / niścakrāma mahattejo brahmaṇaḥ śaṅkarasya ca || 10||',
 'THE ANGER OF THE GODS',
 sc('Vishnu and Shiva frowning, beams of light pouring from their faces, Brahma beside them',GLOW(110,40,40)+GOD(60,140,1,'#ffd60a','#3b6fd0')+GOD(160,140,1,'#fff','#9fd3f0')+GOD(110,150,.7,'#ff8c1a','#e0a800')+'<path d="M64 98 L100 46 M156 98 L120 46 M110 116 V60" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="6"/>'),
 'Hearing these words of the gods, Madhusūdana (Viṣṇu) grew angry, and so did Śambhu (Śiva), their faces twisted with frowning brows. Then from the face of the discus-bearer, filled with great anger, came forth a great radiance, and from Brahmā and Śaṅkara too.',
 [('madhusūdanaḥ','<b>slayer of Madhu</b>: Viṣṇu (Part 6)'),('kopa','<b>anger</b>'),('bhrukuṭī','<b>a frown of the brows</b>'),('mahat tejaḥ','<b>a great radiance</b>')],
 '<b>Anger turned into light.</b> The gods tell Viṣṇu and Śiva everything (2.4–8). Their anger does not become a curse or a war; it becomes <i>tejas</i>, radiant power. Righteous anger, gathered together, becomes something new.',
 'Righteous anger <b>becomes radiant light</b>.'),
VP(C,'11–12','anyeṣāṁ caiva devānāṁ śakrādīnāṁ śarīrataḥ | / nirgataṁ sumahattejastaccaikyaṁ samagacchata || 11|| / atīva tejasaḥ kūṭaṁ jvalantamiva parvatam | / dadṛśuste surāstatra jvālāvyāptadigantaram || 12||',
 'A MOUNTAIN OF LIGHT',
 sc('Streams of light from many gods merging into one blazing mountain of flame that fills the sky',GLOW(110,70,64,'#ff8c1a',.45)+'<path d="M60 120 L110 20 L160 120z" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#e0a800" stroke-width="3"/>'+GODS(156,.4,6,20,36)+''.join(f'<path d="M{20+k*36} 136 L110 80" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(6))),
 'From the bodies of the other gods, Śakra (Indra) and the rest, a very great radiance came forth, and it all became one. The gods saw there a peak of exceeding radiance, like a blazing mountain, its flames filling every direction.',
 [('śakra-ādīnām','<b>of Indra and the others</b>'),('aikyam samagacchata','<b>came into oneness</b>'),('tejasaḥ kūṭam','<b>a peak of radiance</b>'),('jvālā-vyāpta-diganta','<b>flames filling the horizons</b>')],
 '<b>Many lights, one fire.</b> Each god gives his own power, and the powers merge. The Devī of this chapter is literally the <b>combined strength of all the gods</b>: no single god could defeat Mahiṣa, but together, as her, they can.',
 'Every god’s power, <b>merged into one</b>.'),
VP(C,'13','atulaṁ tatra tattejaḥ sarvadevaśarīrajam | / ekasthaṁ tadabhūnnārī vyāptalokatrayaṁ tviṣā || 13||',
 'THE LIGHT BECOMES A WOMAN',
 sc('The blazing light taking the shape of a radiant goddess whose glow fills earth, sky and heaven',GLOW(110,70,76,'#ffd60a',.4)+DEVI(110,0,1.0)+T(30,40,'SVARGA',9)+T(30,90,'BHUVAḤ',9)+T(30,140,'BHŪḤ',9)),
 'That matchless radiance, born from the bodies of all the gods, gathered in one place and became a woman, pervading the three worlds with her lustre.',
 [('atulam','<b>matchless</b>'),('sarva-deva-śarīra-jam','<b>born of the bodies of all gods</b>'),('ekastham','<b>gathered in one</b>'),('nārī','<b>a woman</b>'),('tviṣā','<b>with her lustre</b>')],
 '<b>The birth of the Goddess.</b> This is the verse painted on a thousand Navarātri pandals. In chapter 1 she came out of Viṣṇu as sleep; here she is born as a <b>woman of light</b>, and the second carita’s great form, Mahālakṣmī, stands before the gods.',
 'From all the gods’ light, <b>one Goddess</b>.'),
VP(C,'14–15','yadabhūcchāmbhavaṁ tejastenājāyata tanmukham | / yāmyena cābhavan keśā bāhavo viṣṇutejasā || 14|| / saumyena stanayoryugmaṁ madhyaṁ caindreṇa cābhavat | / vāruṇena ca jaṅghorū nitambastejasā bhuvaḥ || 15||',
 'A BODY MADE OF GODS · I',
 sc('An outline of the Goddess with labels: face from Shiva, hair from Yama, arms from Vishnu, chest from the Moon, waist from Indra, legs from Varuna',DEVI(110,0,.95,True,'#fde4e4')+T(46,40,'ŚIVA → face',9)+T(170,30,'YAMA → hair',9)+T(30,80,'VIṢṆU → arms',9)+T(186,74,'MOON → breast',9)+T(186,110,'INDRA → waist',9)+T(40,140,'VARUṆA → legs',9)+T(180,150,'EARTH → hips',9)),
 'From Śiva’s radiance her face was formed; from Yama’s, her hair; from Viṣṇu’s radiance, her arms. From the Moon’s, her two breasts; from Indra’s, her waist; from Varuṇa’s, her legs and thighs; and from the Earth’s radiance, her hips.',
 [('śāmbhava','<b>of Śambhu</b>: Śiva'),('yāmya','<b>of Yama</b>, lord of death'),('saumya','<b>of Soma</b>, the Moon'),('aindra · vāruṇa','<b>of Indra · of Varuṇa</b>'),('bhuvaḥ tejasā','<b>by the Earth’s radiance</b>')],
 '<b>A map of the body.</b> Each part matches its giver: Śiva, the auspicious, gives the face; Yama, master of time, the dark hair; Viṣṇu, the protector, the many arms; the steady Earth, the hips. The Goddess is a living union of all divine powers.',
 'Each god gives <b>a part of her body</b>.'),
VP(C,'16–18','brahmaṇastejasā pādau tadaṅgulyo’rkatejasā | / vasūnāṁ ca karāṅgulyaḥ kaubereṇa ca nāsikā || 16|| / tasyāstu dantāḥ sambhūtāḥ prājāpatyena tejasā | / nayanatritayaṁ jajñe tathā pāvakatejasā || 17|| / bhruvau ca sandhyayostejaḥ śravaṇāvanilasya ca | / anyeṣāṁ caiva devānāṁ sambhavastejasāṁ śivā || 18||',
 'A BODY MADE OF GODS · II',
 sc('The Goddess’s face close up with three eyes of fire, labels for feet from Brahma, toes from the Sun, fingers from the Vasus, nose from Kubera, ears from the Wind',
  '<circle cx="110" cy="70" r="44" fill="#f2c79b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2.5"/>'+''.join(f'<ellipse cx="{x}" cy="{y}" rx="8" ry="5" fill="#ff8c1a" stroke="#111"/>' for x,y in [(92,66),(128,66),(110,46)])+'<path d="M84 54 q8 -6 16 0 M120 54 q8 -6 16 0" stroke="#111" stroke-width="3" fill="none"/>'+T(110,132,'AGNI → three eyes · SANDHYĀS → brows',9)+T(110,146,'BRAHMĀ → feet · SUN → toes · VASUS → fingers',9)+T(110,158,'KUBERA → nose · PRAJĀPATI → teeth · WIND → ears',9)),
 'From Brahmā’s radiance her feet; from the Sun’s, her toes; from the Vasus’, her fingers; from Kubera’s, her nose. Her teeth arose from the radiance of Prajāpati; her three eyes were born from the radiance of Fire. Her brows came from the radiance of the two twilights, her ears from the Wind’s; and from the radiance of the other gods too, the auspicious one came into being.',
 [('arka','<b>the Sun</b>'),('vasavaḥ','<b>the Vasus</b>, eight gods of the elements'),('kaubera','<b>of Kubera</b>, lord of wealth'),('nayana-tritayam','<b>the three eyes</b>'),('pāvaka','<b>Fire</b>'),('śivā','<b>the auspicious one</b>')],
 '<b>Three eyes of fire.</b> Agni gives her three eyes, like Śiva’s third eye: sun, moon and fire. Her brows come from the two twilights, dawn and dusk. Nothing in her is ordinary; every feature is a god.',
 'Her three eyes are <b>Agni’s fire</b>.'),
VP(C,'20–21','tato devā dadustasyai svāni svānyāyudhāni ca | / śūlaṁ śūlādviniṣkṛṣya dadau tasyai pinākadhṛk ||20|| / cakraṁ ca dattavān kṛṣṇaḥ samutpāṭya svacakrataḥ | / śaṅkhaṁ ca varuṇaḥ śaktiṁ dadau tasyai hutāśanaḥ || 21||',
 'THE GIFTS · TRIDENT AND DISCUS',
 sc('Shiva drawing a second trident out of his own trident, Vishnu drawing a discus out of his discus, Varuna holding out a conch and Agni a spear, all offering them to the Goddess',DEVI(110,0,.7,False)+TRIDENT(30,90,.9)+TRIDENT(44,96,.7)+'<circle cx="190" cy="70" r="14" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="174" cy="82" r="10" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+'<path d="M40 140 q10 -14 22 0 q-10 8 -22 0z" fill="#fff" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M170 150 l20 -40" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="4"/>'+T(50,156,'conch',9)+T(186,156,'śakti',9)),
 'Then the gods gave her each their own weapons. The bearer of Pināka (Śiva) drew a trident out of his own trident and gave it to her. Kṛṣṇa (Viṣṇu) gave her a discus, drawing it out of his own discus. Varuṇa gave her a conch, and Fire (Hutāśana) a spear.',
 [('svāni svāni āyudhāni','<b>each their own weapons</b>'),('śūlāt viniṣkṛṣya','<b>drawing it out of his trident</b>'),('pināka-dhṛk','<b>holder of Pināka</b>: Śiva’s bow'),('śaktiḥ','<b>a spear</b>, Agni’s lance')],
 '<b>Weapon out of weapon.</b> Śiva does not hand over his trident; he draws a new one out of it. Viṣṇu does the same with his discus. Giving to her loses nothing: the source stays full. It is the old Upaniṣadic idea: from fullness, fullness comes.',
 'Weapons drawn <b>out of the gods’ own weapons</b>.'),
VP(C,'22–23','māruto dattavāṁścāpaṁ bāṇapūrṇe tatheṣudhī | / vajramindraḥ samutpāṭya kuliśādamarādhipaḥ || 22|| / dadau tasyai sahasrākṣo ghaṇṭāmairāvatādgajāt | / kāladaṇḍādyamo daṇḍaṁ pāśaṁ cāmbupatirdadau || 23||',
 'THE GIFTS · BOW, THUNDERBOLT, BELL',
 sc('A bow and two full quivers, Indra’s thunderbolt, the bell of the elephant Airavata, Yama’s staff and Varuna’s noose laid out in a row',HIMA+'<path d="M24 40 q-14 40 0 80" fill="none" stroke="#7a5a3a" stroke-width="5"/><path d="M24 40 v80" stroke="#111"/>'+'<rect x="44" y="50" width="12" height="50" fill="#c0392b" stroke="#111"/><rect x="60" y="50" width="12" height="50" fill="#c0392b" stroke="#111"/>'+'<path d="M96 40 l10 30 l-12 0 l12 34" stroke="#e0a800" stroke-width="6" fill="none"/>'+'<path d="M130 96 q10 -36 20 0z" fill="#e0a800" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="140" cy="100" r="4" fill="#111"/>'+'<path d="M168 40 v70" stroke="#3a3a3a" stroke-width="7"/><circle cx="196" cy="74" r="14" fill="none" stroke="#2a6ab0" stroke-width="4"/>'),
 'Māruta (the Wind) gave a bow and two quivers full of arrows. Indra, lord of the immortals, the thousand-eyed, drew a thunderbolt out of his own thunderbolt and gave it, and a bell from his elephant Airāvata. Yama gave a staff out of his staff of death, and the lord of waters (Varuṇa) a noose.',
 [('māruta','<b>the Wind god</b>'),('iṣudhī','<b>two quivers</b>'),('vajra · kuliśa','<b>thunderbolt</b>'),('sahasrākṣa','<b>thousand-eyed</b>: Indra'),('kāla-daṇḍa','<b>the staff of death</b>'),('ambu-pati','<b>lord of waters</b>: Varuṇa')],
 '<b>Why a bell?</b> Among swords and thunderbolts sits Airāvata’s bell. Its sound will stun demons in the battle (2.57). It is also the bell rung in every pūjā to drive away what is not wanted.',
 'Even the pūjā bell <b>is one of her weapons</b>.'),
VP(C,'24','prajāpatiścākṣamālāṁ dadau brahmā kamaṇḍalum | / samastaromakūpeṣu nijaraśmīn divākaraḥ || 24||',
 'THE GIFTS · ROSARY, WATER POT, SUNLIGHT',
 sc('Brahma offering a rosary and water pot, and the Sun pouring rays into every pore of the Goddess’s skin',SUN(170,40,22)+DEVI(110,10,.7,False)+''.join(f'<path d="M170 40 L{90+k*12} {70+(k%3)*20}" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="1.5"/>' for k in range(6))+BRAHMA(40,120,.6)+'<circle cx="74" cy="120" r="12" fill="none" stroke="#7a5a3a" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="2 3"/>'),
 'Brahmā, lord of creatures, gave her a rosary and a water pot. The Sun (Divākara) put his own rays into all the pores of her skin.',
 [('akṣa-mālā','<b>a rosary</b> of beads'),('kamaṇḍalu','<b>a water pot</b>'),('roma-kūpa','<b>the pores of the skin</b>'),('nija-raśmīn','<b>his own rays</b>'),('divākara','<b>maker of the day</b>: the Sun')],
 '<b>Tools of the mind too.</b> Not every gift is a weapon. The rosary and water pot are the tools of prayer and austerity. And the Sun does not hand her anything: he <b>fills her with light</b> from within.',
 'She carries <b>weapons and the rosary</b> alike.'),
VP(C,'30','himavān vāhanaṁ siṁhaṁ ratnāni vividhāni ca | / dadāvaśūnyaṁ surayā pānapātraṁ dhanādhipaḥ || 30||',
 'THE LION FROM THE HIMĀLAYA',
 sc('The snowy Himalaya offering a great lion to the Goddess, and Kubera holding out a jewelled cup',HIMA+LION(110,130,1.1)+DEVI(110,0,.55,False)+'<path d="M178 96 h20 l-4 20 h-12z" fill="#e0a800" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+T(188,132,'Kubera',9)),
 'Himavān (the Himālaya) gave her a lion as her mount, and many kinds of jewels. The lord of wealth (Kubera) gave her a drinking cup ever full of wine.',
 [('himavān','<b>the Himālaya</b>, the mountain king'),('vāhana','<b>a mount</b>'),('siṃha','<b>lion</b>'),('aśūnyam','<b>never empty</b>'),('dhana-adhipa','<b>lord of wealth</b>: Kubera')],
 '<b>The lion.</b> Her mount comes from the Himālaya, the mountain who is also, in other stories, father of Pārvatī. The lion is power under control: wild strength that carries the Goddess wherever she goes. Kubera’s cup will matter in chapter 3.',
 'Her lion <b>comes from the Himālaya</b>.'),
VP(C,'32–33','anyairapi surairdevī bhūṣaṇairāyudhaistathā | / sammānitā nanādoccaiḥ sāṭṭahāsaṁ muhurmuhuḥ || 32|| / tasyā nādena ghoreṇa kṛtsnamāpūritaṁ nabhaḥ | / amāyatātimahatā pratiśabdo mahānabhūt || 33||',
 'THE ROAR',
 sc('The fully armed Goddess on her lion throwing her head back in a great roar, sound waves filling the sky',GLOW(110,50,60,'#ffd60a',.3)+DEVI(110,0,.85)+LION(110,140,.8)+''.join(f'<path d="M{110-30-k*16} {30-k*4} q-10 20 0 40 M{110+30+k*16} {30-k*4} q10 20 0 40" fill="none" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(3))+T(110,16,'HA HA HA!',13,'#c0392b')),
 'Honoured by the other gods too, with ornaments and weapons, the Devī roared aloud with laughter, again and again. The whole sky was filled with her terrible roar, and a great, immeasurable echo arose.',
 [('sammānitā','<b>honoured</b>'),('nanāda uccaiḥ','<b>roared aloud</b>'),('sa-aṭṭahāsam','<b>with loud laughter</b>'),('muhur muhuḥ','<b>again and again</b>'),('pratiśabda','<b>echo</b>')],
 '<b>Laughter as a battle cry.</b> Fully armed, she does not ask for a plan. She laughs. It is the laugh of complete confidence, and it shakes the universe: the next verses say the oceans heaved and mountains swayed.',
 'Her first sound <b>is a fearless laugh</b>.'),
VP(C,'34–35','cukṣubhuḥ sakalā lokāḥ samudrāśca cakampire| / cacāla vasudhā celuḥ sakalāśca mahīdharāḥ || 34|| / jayeti devāśca mudā tāmūcuḥ siṁhavāhinīm | / tuṣṭuvurmunayaścaināṁ bhaktinamrātmamūrtayaḥ || 35||',
 '“JAYA!”',
 sc('Mountains swaying and waves leaping while gods cheer and sages bow to the Goddess on the lion',HIMA+'<path d="M0 130 q27 -18 55 0 t55 0 t55 0 t55 0 V160 H0z" fill="#2a6ab0" stroke="#111"/>'+GODS(146,.5,4,26,24)+SAGE(170,150,.7)+SAGE(196,150,.6)+DEVI(130,10,.45,False)+T(60,40,'JAYA!',16,'#c0392b')),
 'All the worlds trembled, the oceans shook, the earth quaked, and all the mountains swayed. “Victory!” cried the gods in joy to her who rides the lion, and the sages praised her, their bodies bowed in devotion.',
 [('cukṣubhuḥ','<b>were shaken</b>'),('vasudhā','<b>the earth</b>'),('mahīdharāḥ','<b>mountains</b>, “earth-holders”'),('jaya','<b>victory!</b>'),('siṃha-vāhinī','<b>she who rides the lion</b>')],
 '<b>Two reactions to one sound.</b> The worlds shake in fear; the gods shout with joy. The same power is terror to one side and hope to the other. “Jaya” here is the first cheer of a cry still heard at every Navarātri: <i>jaya mātā dī!</i>',
 'Terror for the asuras, <b>joy for the gods</b>.'),
VP(C,'38–39','sa dadarśa tato devīṁ vyāptalokatrayāṁ tviṣā | / pādākrāntyā natabhuvaṁ kirīṭollikhitāmbarām || 38|| / kṣobhitāśeṣapātālāṁ dhanurjyāniḥsvanena tām | / diśo bhujasahasreṇa samantādvyāpya saṁsthitām || 39||',
 'WHAT MAHIṢA SAW',
 sc('A tiny buffalo demon at the bottom looking up at a towering Goddess whose crown scrapes the sky and whose thousand arms fill every direction',NIGHTSKY+STARS(14,3)+''.join(f'<path d="M110 70 l{90*math.cos(k*0.39):.0f} {70*math.sin(k*0.39):.0f}" stroke="#f2c79b" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(16))+DEVI(110,4,.9,False)+'<path d="M0 150 q110 -20 220 0" fill="none" stroke="#c9a26b" stroke-width="4"/>'+BUFFALO(40,146,.35)),
 'Then he saw the Devī, pervading the three worlds with her lustre; the earth bending under the tread of her feet, her crown scraping the sky, the whole underworld shaken by the twang of her bowstring, standing with her thousand arms filling all the directions.',
 [('pāda-ākrāntyā nata-bhuvam','<b>the earth bowed by her tread</b>'),('kirīṭa-ullikhita-ambarām','<b>her crown scraping the sky</b>'),('pātāla','<b>the underworld</b>'),('dhanur-jyā-niḥsvana','<b>the twang of the bowstring</b>'),('bhuja-sahasra','<b>a thousand arms</b>')],
 '<b>The cosmic form.</b> Mahiṣa rushes in shouting “What is this?” (2.37), and sees a Goddess as big as the universe. Like Arjuna seeing Kṛṣṇa’s cosmic form in the Gītā, the sight should have been enough. Pride makes him fight anyway.',
 'He saw a Goddess <b>as big as the universe</b>, and still fought.'),
VP(C,'50–51','sāpi devī tatastāni śastrāṇyastrāṇi caṇḍikā | / līlayaiva praciccheda nijaśastrāstravarṣiṇī || 50 || / anāyastānanā devī stūyamānā surarṣibhiḥ | / mumocāsuradeheṣu śastrāṇyastrāṇi ceśvarī || 51||',
 'AS IF AT PLAY',
 sc('The Goddess calmly cutting a storm of arrows and spears in mid-air with her own, her face relaxed and smiling',BATTLE+DEVI(150,10,.7)+ARROWS(14,90,6)+''.join(f'<path d="M{90+k*6} {50+k*10} l8 -4" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(5))+ASURA(30,150,.8)+ASURA(60,150,.7,'#3a2a6a')+'<path d="M140 56 q10 6 20 0" stroke="#111" fill="none" stroke-width="2"/>'),
 'And Caṇḍikā, raining down her own weapons and missiles, cut all those weapons and missiles to pieces, as if in play. With an untroubled face, praised by gods and sages, the Ruler hurled weapons and missiles onto the bodies of the asuras.',
 [('caṇḍikā','<b>the fierce one</b>: a name of the Devī'),('līlayā eva','<b>as if in play</b>'),('praciccheda','<b>cut to pieces</b>'),('anāyasta-ānanā','<b>with an untroubled face</b>'),('śastra · astra','<b>hand weapons · thrown missiles</b>')],
 '<b>Līlā.</b> The whole war is <i>play</i> to her. Her face shows no strain (anāyasta). This is a key idea: the Divine acts in the world effortlessly, without anxiety. The asuras fight desperately; she fights like someone at play.',
 'She fights <b>without strain, as play</b>.'),
VP(C,'53','niḥśvāsān mumuce yāṁśca yudhyamānā raṇe’mbikā | / ta eva sadyaḥ sambhūtā gaṇāḥ śatasahasraśaḥ || 53||',
 'EVERY BREATH AN ARMY',
 sc('Each breath of the Goddess forming a cloud that turns into rows of small warriors with drums and conches',DEVI(46,20,.6,False)+''.join(f'<path d="M66 44 q{20+k*10} -10 {40+k*20} {-4+k*6}" fill="none" stroke="#9fd3f0" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(3))+''.join(GOD(120+(k%4)*24,90+(k//4)*40,.4,'#ff8c1a','#c98b5a',False) for k in range(8))+T(150,156,'GAṆAS',11)),
 'The breaths that Ambikā breathed out as she fought in battle at once became her troops, in hundreds of thousands.',
 [('niḥśvāsa','<b>out-breath</b>'),('ambikā','<b>Mother</b>'),('sadyaḥ','<b>at once</b>'),('gaṇāḥ','<b>troops, attendants</b>'),('śata-sahasraśaḥ','<b>by hundreds of thousands</b>')],
 '<b>She needs no allies.</b> The gods gave her weapons, but her army comes from her own breath. Her gaṇas fight beside her and even play drums and conches “in that great festival of war” (2.55).',
 'Her very breath <b>becomes her army</b>.'),
]
